From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 16:50:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: venus: msm8998: restore the TZ threshold registers after
 firmware boot

After a power-collapse resume the firmware never answers another HFI
message: TZ accepts the resume (scm_set_remote_state(1) = 0), the ARM9
comes back and sets INIT_DONE, and then SYS_SESSION_INIT gets silence,
traced with hfi_trace on both sides.

The vendor names the cause in __set_threshold_registers(): on Venus
3.43 -- the msm8998 part, WRAPPER_HW_VERSION 0x3043xxxx -- registers
normally programmed by TZ come out of a firmware reboot incorrectly
reset, and TZBSP video state 2 (RESTORE_THRESHOLD) is the agreed
handshake that reprograms them. Downstream issues it after every
__boot_firmware() in __resume(), and again on secure
LOAD_RESOURCES_DONE. Mainline never sends state 2 at all.

Issue it after every successful venus_boot_core() on matching hardware,
exactly as the vendor does, ignoring the return value the same way
(a cold-boot TZ may refuse it; that is harmless and the vendor treats
it as best-effort too).

With this, a decode session opened after a runtime suspend/resume cycle
works, where before it timed out at SESSION_INIT.

Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/hfi_venus.c | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/hfi_venus.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/venus/hfi_venus.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/interrupt.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
+#include <linux/firmware/qcom/qcom_scm.h>
 #include <linux/kernel.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 
@@ -567,6 +568,18 @@
 
 	venus_hwversion(hdev);
 
+	/*
+	 * Venus 3.43 (msm8998): registers normally programmed by TZ come out
+	 * of a firmware reboot incorrectly reset, and TZBSP video state 2
+	 * (RESTORE_THRESHOLD) is the agreed handshake that reprograms them.
+	 * The vendor issues it after every __boot_firmware() -- without it the
+	 * firmware never answers another HFI message after a power-collapse
+	 * resume. Cold boots tolerate it; any TZ refusal is not fatal.
+	 */
+	if ((readl(hdev->core->wrapper_base + WRAPPER_HW_VERSION) &
+	     ~GENMASK(15, 0)) == 0x30430000)
+		qcom_scm_set_remote_state(2, 0);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
